---
// What coding.sh can't do yet, and what to use until it can. The agent lists come
// from components/docs/agents.ts, so they change when an adapter lands or is run
// live. A line that calls something planned must name a feature in FEATURES
// (test/status-claims.test.mjs), which fails once the code is there.
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import { ADAPTER_ROWS, PLANNED_AGENTS } from "../../components/docs/agents";
import { ISSUES_URL } from "../../data/site";

const list = (names: readonly string[]) =>
  names.length < 2 ? names.join("") : `${names.slice(0, -1).join(", ")} and ${names[names.length - 1]}`;
const noAdapter = list(PLANNED_AGENTS);
const notLive = ADAPTER_ROWS.filter((a) => a.status === "not-live");
const AGENT_ISSUE = `${ISSUES_URL}/new?template=agent-support.yml`;
const wrap = `coding.sh run --agent my-agent -- ./my-agent --task 42`;
---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    Each entry says what to use in the meantime. Something you need that isn't here? Open a
    <a href={`${ISSUES_URL}/new?template=feature.yml`}>feature request</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="adapters">Agents with no adapter</h2>
  <p>
    {noAdapter} have no adapter, so coding.sh doesn't read their hooks or transcripts. Until one lands, record a run with
    <code>coding.sh run --</code>. It keeps the command as a session with its duration, its exit code and what changed in the repo:
  </p>
  <Code code={wrap} />
  <p>
    To see the agent's model calls as well, point its API base URL at <a href="/docs/how-to/capture-with-proxy">the proxy</a>, if the
    agent lets you set one. To ask for an adapter, open an <a href={AGENT_ISSUE}>agent support</a> issue.
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-live">Adapters not run live</h2>
  <p>
    Nobody has run these adapters on a live install yet:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      notLive.map((a) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{a.name}.</strong> {a.note ? `${a.note[0]!.toUpperCase()}${a.note.slice(1)}.` : null}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you use one of them, run <code>coding.sh doctor</code> after a session and tell us what it shows in an
    <a href={AGENT_ISSUE}>agent support</a> issue. A short "it works" helps as much as a bug report.
  </p>

  <h2 id="windows">Capture on Windows</h2>
  <p>
    On Windows, the installer has been checked, and so has capture from OpenCode, Pi, Goose, Factory Droid, GitHub Copilot CLI and
    Aider. Capture from Claude Code, Codex CLI, Antigravity CLI, Cursor CLI, Amp and T3 Code has not been checked on Windows.
    Under WSL, coding.sh works as it does on Linux.
  </p>

  <h2 id="repo-tasks">Repo tasks from hook sessions</h2>
  <p>
    A session recorded by hooks becomes a repo task from the commit that was checked out when it started. Changes that were not committed
    at that point are missing, and <code>coding.sh eval new</code> says so. For the exact starting state, record the run with
    <code>coding.sh run --</code>, which snapshots the whole working tree. See <a href="/docs/how-to/session-to-eval">Turn failed runs into
    evals</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bench">One <code>bench</code> command and <code>coding.sh try</code></h2>
  <p>
    One <code>bench</code> command over evals, experiments and agent comparisons, and <code>coding.sh try</code> for a first run. Today
    <code>coding.sh eval</code>, <code>coding.sh exp</code> and <code>coding.sh compare</code> do this work, and <code>coding.sh bench</code>
    runs <a href="/docs/benchmarks">benchmarks</a> on fresh cases.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
